PROPRIETARY LICENCE - FREE AT HOME, PAID AT WORK

In one line: run it at home for nothing, pay for it if your organisation
benefits from it, and if you ever improve it, offer the improvement back.

1. WHAT YOU MAY DO AT NO CHARGE

You may download, install and run BondVPN, on any number of machines you own or
administer personally, for:

  a. personal and household use;
  b. education, teaching and academic research;
  c. evaluation within an organisation, for up to 30 days, to decide whether to
     licence it.

You may keep copies and backups for your own use.

2. WHAT REQUIRES A PAID LICENCE

Any other use is commercial use, and needs a licence agreed with the copyright
holder in writing. Commercial use includes, without limitation:

  a. use in or for the benefit of a business, government body or other
     organisation, beyond the 30-day evaluation above;
  b. providing a service to other people that relies on BondVPN, whether or not
     those people are charged;
  c. including BondVPN, or anything containing it, in a product, appliance or
     paid offering.

To arrange a commercial licence, open an issue on the project's repository.

3. IMPROVEMENTS COME BACK

BondVPN is distributed as binaries; no source code is published, so in normal
use there is nothing for you to modify. If source code is ever made available
to you, then for any changes you make:

  a. you must offer those changes back to the copyright holder, in source form,
     before or when you first rely on them beyond your own machine;
  b. you grant the copyright holder a perpetual, worldwide, irrevocable,
     royalty-free, sublicensable licence to use, modify and distribute those
     changes as part of BondVPN, under any terms;
  c. you keep your own copyright in what you wrote. This is a licence to the
     copyright holder, not a transfer of ownership.

You may not distribute a modified version to anyone else without written
permission.

4. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

  a. redistribute, sublicense, rent, lease or sell the software, or make it
     available to third parties as part of a paid service, without written
     permission;
  b. remove, disable or work around the licence check described below;
  c. remove or alter copyright notices, including this file;
  d. decompile, disassemble or otherwise reverse engineer the software, except
     to the extent that this restriction cannot lawfully be excluded.

5. THE LICENCE CHECK

BondVPN fetches a small signed status file once an hour to confirm this
installation is still licensed. The request carries no identifier of any kind -
no install ID, no address, no telemetry - and nothing about it distinguishes
your copy from anyone else's. It is documented in the README rather than left
to be discovered.

If no valid status has been seen for 24 hours, BondVPN withdraws the client
routing and exits, leaving its kill switch armed. A stopped installation goes
quiet; it never opens a path around your tunnels.

